<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<!-- The floor and the newest, and nothing between: a parser here is ordinary C#
		     over a generated one, so netstandard2.0 is what every consumer can read and
		     net10.0 is what a consumer on the newest runtime gets without the shims. -->
		<TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<RootNamespace>DotGram.Sql</RootNamespace>

		<Description>SQL parsers written in .gram: ISO SQL:2023, SQL-92 and Microsoft's T-SQL, all three building one tree of the standard's own constructs, with T-SQL's beside them.</Description>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>parser;sql;iso-sql;sql-2023;sql-92;tsql;t-sql;sql-server;dotgram</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>Since 0.2.0. The three parsers build one tree: TransactSqlParser and Sql92Parser now build DotGram.Sql.Ast, the tree SqlStandardParser builds, and the T-SQL tree of 0.2.0 is gone. A script is cut into batches as sqlcmd cuts it, and every parser is carried immediately. docs/ast.md maps every node to the production or page it comes from.

BREAKING CHANGES, and what to write instead

- One tree. TransactSqlParser and Sql92Parser hand back DotGram.Sql.Ast nodes through every entry, plain and Located: a Statement, a Statement[], an Expression or a Query of DotGram.Sql.Ast. A text two parsers read is the same tree. The 0.2.0 T-SQL tree in the DotGram.Sql namespace is removed: Statement, SetExpression, Query, Expression, TableReference, Clause, their records and factories, SqlComparison and SqlTruth; SqlJoin, SqlOrder, SqlLiteralKind and SetCategory are no longer public. Migration: use the types of DotGram.Sql.Ast; walk with SqlWalker; write with SqlWriter or TransactSqlWriter. A T-SQL construct with no node of its own yet comes back as an Extension node (Dialect "T-SQL", and a Kind that names it), which the writers refuse.
- Writers. Sql2023Writer is renamed SqlWriter, in DotGram.Sql.Ast; the old SqlWriter of the DotGram.Sql namespace, which wrote only the removed tree, is gone. Migration: DotGram.Sql.Ast.Sql2023Writer.Write -&gt; DotGram.Sql.Ast.SqlWriter.Write. The writers print key words and date parts in capitals.
- Batch moves from DotGram.Sql to DotGram.Sql.Ast, with Statements, Go and Source. Migration: change the using.
- Entries. SqlStandardParser reads by level, six entries where it published forty-two productions: ParseValue (was ParseLiteral; also reads NULL), ParseDataType, ParseExpression (was ParseValueExpression), ParseSearchCondition, ParseStatement (replaces ParseDirectSQLStatement, ParseSQLProcedureStatement and ParseQueryExpression) and ParseSql; 258 public methods are removed. TransactSqlParser reads ParseExpression (was ParseValueExpression), ParseSearchCondition, ParseStatement, ParseSql and ParseSelect, with their per-version and Try forms; ParseQuery is gone. Migration: read a production below a level through the level that holds it (a table reference as a query that names it, a predicate as a search condition); for a T-SQL query call ParseSelect and take its Query.
- Lists. Every list a node holds is a SqlList&lt;T&gt;, a read-only struct over one array that compares element by element: 183 IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt; members of DotGram.Sql.Ast. Reading code mostly compiles unchanged (foreach, an indexer, Length, Count, LINQ, list patterns). Migration: where a list is handed in, write [.. array] or SqlList.From(array); use the list's own Exists and TrueForAll, or AsSpan, in place of Array.Exists and the other T[] methods; a SqlList&lt;Derived&gt; is not a SqlList&lt;Base&gt;.
- Equality by content. A node's Span takes no part in its equality: a located tree equals the plain tree of the same text, and two equal subtrees in different places compare equal. Migration: a table keyed by node is keyed by reference (ReferenceEqualityComparer). Hash values differ from 0.2.0.
- QualifiedName is a struct, its first Identifier and an array of the others: an optional name is a QualifiedName?, a Nullable, whose value is x.Value; the positional Parts and its Deconstruct are gone (Parts is kept and makes a list on each call; Count, the indexer, First, Last and foreach read without allocating). default(QualifiedName) is no name, and the writers and SqlWalker refuse it.
- LiteralValue is an Expression, and Expression.Literal is removed. Migration: Expression.Literal { Value: LiteralValue.Numeric n } -&gt; LiteralValue.Numeric n; new Expression.Literal(v) -&gt; v.
- Scripts. ParseScript, TryParseScript and their levels cut a script as ODBC sqlcmd 18.6 cuts it, so they answer differently wherever 0.2.0 disagreed with sqlcmd: GO; is text, a GO after a statement on its line is text, GO /* c */, GO 2; and GO x are refused at that line, :setvar is read and $(name) substituted, :r is refused. The four position and window forms of TryParseScript are removed at every level, 72 methods. Migration: cut the text with SqlScript.Read and read each batch with TryParseSql(ScriptBatch). ParseSql no longer refuses a GO line: in one command GO is a word, as the server reads it. Text from a file or an editor is a script, for ParseScript; text a program sends in one command is for ParseSql.
- Sql92Parser reserves every word SQL-92 reserves (section 5.2), so year, month, zone, with, names and the like are refused as names - write them delimited ("year"); SELECT b DAY FROM t reads b qualified by DAY. It refuses an interval qualifier SQL-92 does not have and a length, precision or scale above 2,147,483,647.
- Syntax, the helpers the T-SQL grammar's rules call, is no longer public.
- In a located T-SQL reading, an OVER spans the keyword and the window's name or brackets, as a located reading of SQL:2023 spans it.

NEW

- SqlScript.Read(text, options) cuts a script into the batches a client tool sends: ScriptProfile.SqlCmd() reads separator lines, GO n, :setvar and $(name), :r through ScriptOptions.ResolveInclude (bounded by ScriptOptions.MaximumIncludes) and reports every other command as a ScriptDirective; ScriptProfile.Ssms() reads separator lines alone. Each ScriptBatch has Count, IsVerbatim and Locate. TransactSqlParser.TryParseSql(ScriptBatch) and its forms read one batch. Held byte for byte to what sqlcmd 18.6 sends the server.
- SqlList&lt;T&gt;, the list every node holds, made with a collection expression, SqlList.From or ToSqlList(). A list of one element of a reference type holds it without an array.
- TransactSqlParser.Located is the same parser called with locations on, rather than a second compilation, so the assembly carries one T-SQL parser instead of two.

CHANGED

- All three parsers are carried immediately (Carrier = GramCarrier.Immediate): a node is built as its rule is read. Accepted readings are faster; a text refused part-way allocates more. Trees, refusals and messages are as before.
- A node of DotGram.Sql.Ast keeps its location in eight bytes (start and length); its Span.GapStart is its At.
- A located node's span is the range of the rule that built it; a clause's keyword is no longer inside the value it introduces (a WHERE condition spans its own text).
- Equality and GetHashCode of a node throw InsufficientExecutionStackException on a tree deeper than the stack has room for, where they would have overflowed it.

FIXED

- Ten thousand nested T-SQL CASE … ELSE CASE … ended the process with a stack overflow; they are read. The writers carry a deep walk onto a thread of their own, and the T-SQL checks of a chain of UNION, brackets and PIVOT loop rather than recurse.
- SqlStandardParser refuses a length, precision, scale or cardinality beyond 2147483647 and a large object length beyond 9223372036854775807, where it read CHAR (4294967297) as 1 and threw OverflowException on a number beyond long.
- SqlStandardParser no longer reads a string literal through a simple comment: 'a' --c 'b' is the literal 'a' and a comment.
- TransactSqlParser refuses EXTRACT(f FROM x), POSITION(a IN b), SUBSTRING(x FROM a FOR b), CONVERT(x USING cs), TRANSLATE(x USING cs), CURRENT_TIME, CURRENT_TIME(n) and CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(n), which it read from SQL-92's rules and SQL Server 2025 refuses at every compatibility level. CURRENT_DATE and plain CURRENT_TIMESTAMP stay, and so do the ODBC escapes { fn EXTRACT (HOUR FROM d) } and { fn CURRENT_TIME }.

KNOWN ISSUES

- TODO(freeze): T-SQL speed and allocation against 0.2.0, one line from the release benchmark (the one tree costs bytes on some rows).</PackageReleaseNotes>

		<!-- What a caller reads in the editor. The other packages here fail a build on a public
		     member without a comment; this one cannot yet, because the tree that is leaving has four
		     hundred records and none of them is documented (docs/design/sql-tsql-tree.md). So the
		     documentation ships and CS1591 is off: a node written from here on carries its summary and
		     the page of the specification it comes from, and the day the count reaches zero the NoWarn
		     goes and the compiler keeps it at zero. Turning it on now rather than then is what makes
		     the new nodes visible to IntelliSense while they are being written. -->
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<!-- The two that say a comment is MISSING: CS1591 for a public member without one, CS1573 for
		     a record that documents some of its parameters and not the rest. Both are about coverage,
		     and both are the old tree's. The ones that say a comment is WRONG stay on: CS1570 for
		     malformed XML, CS1574 for a cref that resolves to nothing, because those are defects in
		     what a reader is told rather than gaps in what they are told. Turning the documentation on
		     found six of CS1570 in the summaries of SqlAst.cs, where a BNF production name was
		     written in angle brackets and is not XML. -->
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>

		<!-- Symbols inside the assembly, as the generator carries them: a stack trace from
		     a parser here has line numbers without a symbol server. -->
		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>

		<!-- Paths in the PDB and the package normalized to the repository rather than the
		     runner's disk, so that what CI packs can be rebuilt byte for byte from the commit
		     its nuspec names. Here and not in Directory.Build.props: it rewrites every source
		     path, [CallerFilePath] included, to /_/, and nine test classes find the
		     repository from their own file — set for everything, it failed eighty of them.
		     Only on CI, because locally it would point a debugger at /_/. -->
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

		<!-- Generated files land on disk so a reader can open what the grammars became. -->
		<EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
		<CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)GeneratedFiles</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The generator, and nothing else: a .gram parser acquires no runtime
		     dependency, which is why this can be an ordinary library (docs/syntax.md
		     §6.1). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\DotGram\DotGram.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(DotGramAnalyzeGenerated)' == 'true'">
		<!-- The generator's own output, analysed for dead or redundant emitted code
		     (tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers), never packed, loaded only under this
		     property (docs/development.md). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="../../tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- What a consumer on this framework needs: the generated parser's own methods
		     take `ReadOnlySpan<char>`, which is not in the framework there. It is the one
		     dependency this package has, and it has it only here. -->
		<PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />

		<!-- And what the compilation needs rather than the consumer: the records written
		     by hand beside the grammars have `init` accessors, and `IsExternalInit` is not
		     declared on this framework. The polyfill is a generator and emits it as an
		     internal type, so nothing reaches the package. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- `required` members, which the SQL:2023 tree's records declare, need two more, and
		     SqlList<T>'s collection expressions need CollectionBuilderAttribute. -->
		<MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IsExternalInit;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RequiredMemberAttribute;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CompilerFeatureRequiredAttribute;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CollectionBuilderAttribute;T:System.Index;T:System.Range;T:System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis;M:System.Enum.GetValues;M:System.Collections.Generic.List`1.Slice(System.Int32,System.Int32);M:System.String.StartsWith(System.Char;M:System.String.EndsWith(System.Char;M:System.String.Contains(System.Char);M:System.String.Split(System.Char;M:System.String.Join(System.Char;M:System.String.Join``1(System.Char;M:System.String.Replace(System.String,System.String,System.StringComparison)</MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- What the package says for itself. The grammars are in the repository and the
		     README points at them: a parser here is worth reading as much as it is worth
		     calling. -->
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />

		<!-- And the skill beside it, so that an agent in a project that has restored this
		     package can read how to use it without leaving the machine. The README says
		     where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The public surface, held to PublicAPI.*.txt beside this file, so that a type or a member made public, or
		     changed, is a line in a diff and not an accident. What only one framework has is in PublicAPI/<framework>/:
		     a record's clone returns its own type on net10.0 and the base type on netstandard2.0, which has no covariant
		     returns. Unshipped until the package ships with it. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
		<AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI/$(TargetFramework)/PublicAPI.*.txt" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The grammars as files the generator reads: a .gram beside the class that hosts
		     it, so that another project can point the generator at the same text and compile
		     it another way — which is what DotGram.Benchmarks does with the SQL grammar. -->
		<AdditionalFiles Include="Standard\SqlStandard.gram" />
		<AdditionalFiles Include="Standard\SqlStandard92.gram" />
		<AdditionalFiles Include="TransactSql\TransactSql.gram" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- For the benchmark host that compiles SqlStandard.gram on another carrier; Nodes and Towers stay internal. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Benchmarks" />

		<!-- For the test fixture that includes SqlStandard.gram and publishes its productions one by one
		     (tests/DotGram.Sql.Productions): the included rules call Nodes and Towers. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Sql.Productions" />

		<!-- For the tests that hold T-SQL's builders (TransactSql/Building) and the records its rules read, both internal. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Sql.Tests" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
